IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wills-dates`
IMPORT `wills-rules`

-- Wills Act 1997 (Vic) -- the questions of the WILL-MAKER.
--
-- SEAT: the individual making, changing or revoking a will in Victoria (the testator), including a minor who wants to make
-- one and a person whose will was made for them by Court order and who has regained capacity. "Can I make a valid will?",
-- "Is my will properly signed and witnessed?", "Does my marriage revoke my will?", "What does my divorce do to my will?",
-- "Is my change to my will effective?", "Have I revoked my will?". The Court and the Registrar get no seat: their
-- satisfactions and orders are asked as facts. The family's questions after a death are in wills-seat-family.l4.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of wills-rules.l4.
-- No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Wills Act 1997,
-- Version No. 036 (26 April 2021), not the authorised version. (c) State of Victoria; published with the authority of the
-- Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be reproduced except in accordance with the Copyright Act 1968
-- (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Wills Act 1997: the will-maker's questions`

§§ `Who may make a will`

@export Is the will I am making valid as far as my age goes (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 5, 6, 20)? A will made by someone under 18 is not valid unless they are married, make it in contemplation of a marriage that then takes place, or the Supreme Court authorises it. Being of sound mind (testamentary capacity) is a separate, common law test not answered here.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years on the day you make the will
      `status` IS A `Marital status` @desc Your marital status when you make the will: never married; married now; or married before but not now (divorced or widowed)
      `contemplation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are making the will in contemplation of a particular marriage you are planning
      `court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made an order under section 20 authorising you to make this will in these terms
GIVETH A `Whether my will is valid as to my age`
`Is my will valid as to my age` MEANS
    `ss 5, 6, 20 -- my will is valid as to my age` `age` `status` `contemplation` `court order`

@export Can I revoke my will although I am under 18 (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 6, 20)? Yes if you are married, if the will was made in contemplation of marriage, if you have been married and the will was made while you were married, or with a Supreme Court order. Otherwise the Act does not say.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years now
      `status` IS A `Marital status` @desc Your marital status now: never married; married now; or married before but not now (divorced or widowed)
      `made in contemplation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will you want to revoke was made in contemplation of a marriage
      `made while married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will you want to revoke was made while you were married
      `court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made an order under section 20 authorising you to revoke it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I revoke my will as a minor` MEANS
    `ss 6, 20 -- I may revoke my will although a minor` `age` `status` `made in contemplation` `made while married` `court order`

@export I am under 18: can the Supreme Court authorise me to make (or revoke) a will in specific terms (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 20)? You or someone on your behalf may apply; the Registrar must be one of the witnesses, and the will is deposited with the Registrar.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years now
      `understand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied you understand the nature and effect of the proposed will or revocation and the extent of the property it disposes of
      `reflects` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the proposed will or revocation accurately reflects your intentions
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied it is reasonable in all the circumstances to make the order
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court authorise my will as a minor` MEANS
    `s 20 -- the Court may authorise my will as a minor` `age` `understand` `reflects` `reasonable`

§§ `Signing and witnessing my will`

@export Is my will validly signed and witnessed in person (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 7, 8, 10)? It must be in writing, signed by you (or by someone in your presence at your direction) intending it as your will, with two or more witnesses present together who can see you sign, and at least two of them must sign in your presence. A witness need not know it is a will, and the signature need not be at the foot.
GIVEN `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is in writing (on paper or other material)
      `who signed` IS A `Who signed the will` @desc Who signed the will: you; someone else in your presence and at your direction; someone else not in your presence or not at your direction; or nobody
      `intending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the signature was made with the intention of executing the document as your will
      `witnesses present` IS A NUMBER @desc How many witnesses, each able to see you sign, were present at the same time when you signed or acknowledged your signature
      `witnesses signed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those witnesses then signed the will in your presence (they need not sign in each other's presence)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my will validly signed and witnessed in person` MEANS
    `s 7(1), 10 -- my will is validly executed in person` `in writing` `who signed` `intending` `witnesses present` `witnesses signed`

@export Is my will validly signed and witnessed over a video link, by the remote execution procedure (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 7(5)-(7), 8A, 19E(2))? One witness must be a special witness (a lawyer or a justice of the peace), all steps must happen on the same day and in Victoria, and the special witness signs last and adds a statement. Not for an international will.
GIVEN `international` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is meant to be an international will under the Washington Convention (the Schedule to the Act)
      `special witness` IS A `Kind of special witness` @desc Which special witness was one of the witnesses: an Australian legal practitioner; a justice of the peace; a member of a class the regulations prescribe; or no special witness
      `witnesses` IS A NUMBER @desc How many witnesses took part, counting the special witness
      `same day in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every step was done on the same day and within Victoria
      `all saw` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every witness clearly saw your signature (or your substitute signatory's, made at your direction they heard) being made, in person or by audio visual link
      `special last` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the special witness signed last
      `link witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each witness on the link was satisfied it was the same document, put on it a statement that they witnessed it by audio visual link under the procedure, and signed while you watched (answer yes if every witness was physically present)
      `special statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the special witness checked the will and put on it a statement that it was signed and witnessed under the procedure, what kind of special witness they are, and whether a recording was made
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my will validly signed and witnessed remotely` MEANS
    `ss 7(5), 8A, 19E(2) -- my will is validly executed by the remote execution procedure` `international` `special witness` `witnesses` `same day in Victoria` `all saw` `special last` `link witnesses` `special statement`

@export If a witness to my will (or their spouse or domestic partner) is a beneficiary, can they still take the gift (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 11)?
GIVEN `witness benefits` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a witness to the will, or the witness's spouse or domestic partner when the will was witnessed, is given something by the will
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can a witness who benefits keep the gift` MEANS
    `s 11 -- a witness who benefits keeps the gift` `witness benefits`

@export I made my will outside Victoria (or under another country's rules): is it taken to be properly executed (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 17)? Yes if its execution follows the law of the place where it was signed, of your domicile or habitual residence, or of a country you are a national of (when made or at death), or for land, the law where the land is.
GIVEN `place executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether its execution follows the law of the place where you signed it
      `domicile` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether its execution follows the law of the place that was your domicile or habitual residence, when you made it or at your death
      `nationality` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether its execution follows the law of a country you were a national of, when you made it or at your death
      `land` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a gift of land, whether its execution follows the law of the place where the land is
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my will made abroad properly executed` MEANS
    `s 17 -- my will is taken to be properly executed under a foreign law` `place executed` `domicile` `nationality` `land`

§§ `Changing my will`

@export Is the change I made to my will after signing it effective (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 15)? A change is effective only if signed and witnessed as a will must be; signatures in the margin or beside the change, or on a note about it written on the will, are enough.
GIVEN `after` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the change after the will was signed and witnessed
      `executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you signed the change and two witnesses signed it, as a will must be signed and witnessed (in the margin, beside it, or on a note on the will about it)
      `obliterated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the original words can no longer be read because of the change (for example, fully blacked out)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my change to my will effective` MEANS
    `s 15 -- my alteration is effective` `after` `executed` `obliterated`

@export Is the clause in my will letting my executor charge for their work valid (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 49A)? For a will signed on or after 1 November 2017, the clause is void unless you gave written informed consent to it before signing the will.
GIVEN `executed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you signed the will
      `consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave written informed consent to including the clause
      `before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave that written consent before you signed the will
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the charging clause in my will valid` MEANS
    `s 49A -- the remuneration clause in my will is not void` `executed on` `consent` `before`

§§ `Revoking my will`

@export Have I revoked my will by what I did (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 12)? A will is revoked only by a later will, by a signed and witnessed writing saying so, by destroying it intending to revoke it, or by writing on or dealing with it so that the Court is satisfied from its state that you meant to revoke it (by you, or someone in your presence at your direction). Marriage and divorce are separate questions.
GIVEN `how` IS A `How I tried to revoke the will` @desc What you did: made a later will; signed a writing declaring you revoke it; burned, tore or otherwise destroyed it; wrote on it or otherwise dealt with it; or something else (told someone, or wrote something not signed and witnessed)
      `executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a later will or a writing: whether it was signed and witnessed as a will must be
      `later will revokes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a later will: whether it says it revokes earlier wills, or is inconsistent with this one
      `who` IS A `Who did the act of revoking` @desc Who did it: you; someone else in your presence and at your direction; or someone else otherwise
      `intending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was done with the intention of revoking the will
      `court satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For writing on or dealing with the will: whether the Court is satisfied, from the state of the will, that you intended to revoke it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Have I revoked my will` MEANS
    `s 12 -- my will is revoked by what I did` `how` `executed` `later will revokes` `who` `intending` `court satisfied`

@export Does my marriage, or a new relationship, revoke my will (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 12, 13)? Marriage revokes a will unless it was made in contemplation of that marriage or of marriage generally; gifts to and appointments of the person you are married to at your death survive. A domestic relationship does not revoke a will.
GIVEN `relationship` IS A `A new relationship since the will` @desc What has happened since you made the will (or revived it): nothing; you married; or you began a domestic relationship (registered, or living together as a couple) without marrying
      `this marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the will in contemplation of this particular marriage (whether or not the will says so)
      `marriage generally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will says it is made in contemplation of marriage generally
GIVETH A `What the new relationship does to my will`
`Does my marriage revoke my will` MEANS
    `ss 12, 13 -- what my marriage or new relationship does to my will` `relationship` `this marriage` `marriage generally`

@export Does my divorce revoke this part of my will that names my former spouse (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 12, 14)? A divorce or annulment revokes gifts to a former spouse and their appointment as executor, trustee or guardian, and powers of appointment for them, unless it appears you wanted them kept; the will then reads as if they had died before you. Separation alone, or the end of a domestic relationship, revokes nothing.
GIVEN `ending` IS A `How the relationship ended` @desc How the relationship ended: a divorce under the Family Law Act 1975 took effect; the Family Court annulled the marriage; it was dissolved or annulled overseas in a way recognised in Australia; you separated without a divorce or annulment; or a domestic relationship (not a marriage) ended
      `will existed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was already made when the divorce or annulment took effect
      `part` IS A `Part of my will that names my former spouse` @desc Which part of the will you are asking about: a gift to your former spouse; their appointment as executor; as trustee; as trustee of property left on trust for beneficiaries who include their children; as advisory trustee or guardian; a power of appointment exercisable by or for them; or a power exercisable by them only for your joint children
      `wanted kept` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears (from the will or other evidence) that you did not want this part revoked when the marriage ended
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my divorce revoke this part of my will` MEANS
    `ss 12, 14 -- my divorce revokes this part of my will` `ending` `will existed` `part` `wanted kept`

@export Is my revoked will revived (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 16)? A revoked will is revived by signing it again with witnesses, or by a codicil showing you mean to revive it; the revived will is taken to be made on the day of revival.
GIVEN `re-executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you signed the revoked will again with two witnesses, as a will must be signed and witnessed
      `codicil` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made a properly signed and witnessed codicil showing an intention to revive the will
      `partly then wholly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was first revoked in part and later revoked as to the rest
      `contrary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document that revives it shows an intention that the whole will, not only the part most recently revoked, be revived
GIVETH A `What revives my revoked will`
`Is my revoked will revived` MEANS
    `s 16 -- what revives my revoked will` `re-executed` `codicil` `partly then wholly` `contrary`

§§ `A will the Court authorised for me`

-- This group arises only for a person whose will was made by Court order under s 21 while they lacked testamentary
-- capacity: ask "Was your will made for you by order of the Supreme Court?" before showing it.

@export My will was made for me by Supreme Court order while I lacked capacity: can I now revoke or change it myself (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 24)?
GIVEN `regained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have acquired or regained testamentary capacity since the order was made
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I change my Court-authorised will myself` MEANS
    `s 24 -- I may revoke or change my Court-authorised will myself` `regained`
